# The Interior of the Earth: Layers and Sources of Evidence

> No drill has ever reached the mantle, yet geographers map the deep
> Earth with confidence. This lesson explains the evidence behind that
> knowledge and the layered structure it reveals.

## 1. The Challenge of Depth

The Earth's mean radius is about **6,371 km**, but the deepest human
excavation is barely a scratch on that scale. Direct observation stops
a few kilometres below the surface, so the interior must be pieced
together from indirect clues.

[important title="Scale check"]
If the Earth were an apple, the deepest mine and the deepest borehole
combined would not even pierce the skin. Humans have directly sampled
well under 1% of the way to the planet's centre.
[/important]

## 2. Direct and Indirect Sources of Evidence

| Source | Kind | What it reveals |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deep mines (deepest: Mponeng, South Africa, ~4 km) | Direct | Rock samples, temperature, and pressure near the surface |
| Superdeep drilling (Kola Borehole, ~12 km) | Direct | Crustal rocks and the geothermal gradient |
| Volcanic eruptions | Indirect | Molten magma rising from the mantle |
| Meteorites | Indirect | Analogues of the core and of the early planet |
| Seismic waves | Indirect | The most important source: layer depths, states, densities |

[definition]
**Seismic waves** are vibrations that travel through the Earth after an
earthquake or explosion. Their speed and path change whenever they cross
a boundary between materials, so they scan the planet like a natural
X-ray machine.
[/definition]

## 3. The Three Main Layers

@diagram tree id="earth-interior-layers" layout="tree" aria="Earth divided into crust, mantle, and core"
  Earth -> Crust
  Earth -> Mantle
  Earth -> Core
  Crust -> "Continental crust (sial)"
  Crust -> "Oceanic crust (sima)"
  Core -> "Outer core (liquid)"
  Core -> "Inner core (solid)"
@/diagram

| Layer | Thickness | State and composition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Crust | Continental 30–70 km; oceanic 5–10 km | Solid silicate rock |
| Mantle | About 2,900 km thick | Solid but slowly flowing silicate rock |
| Outer core | About 2,200 km thick | Liquid iron and nickel |
| Inner core | About 1,220 km radius | Solid iron–nickel sphere |

[note]
The **sial** (silica + aluminium) of the continents is lighter than the
**sima** (silica + magnesium) of the ocean floor, which is why
continents stand high while ocean basins sit low.
[/note]

## 4. Discontinuities: The Boundaries Within

A **discontinuity** is a depth at which seismic waves abruptly change
speed, marking a change of material.

| Discontinuity | Depth | Separates |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Conrad | ~20 km | Upper and lower continental crust |
| Mohorovicic (Moho), found 1909 | Base of the crust | Crust and mantle |
| Repetti | ~700 km | Upper and lower mantle |
| Gutenberg | ~2,900 km | Mantle and outer core |
| Lehmann, 1936 | ~5,100 km | Liquid outer core and solid inner core |

