## 5. Temperature, Pressure, and Density

All three properties climb steeply with depth.

| Property | Near the surface | Near the centre |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Temperature | ~15°C annual mean | 5,000–6,000°C |
| Pressure | 1 atmosphere | Over 3.5 million atmospheres |
| Density | ~2.7 g/cm³ (crustal rock) | ~13.6 g/cm³ (inner core) |

[warning]
The geothermal gradient of roughly 1°C per 32 m of descent holds only
near the surface; it cannot be simply extended to the centre of the
Earth.
[/warning]

## 6. Lithosphere and Asthenosphere

A second, mechanical division cuts across the chemical one:

- **Lithosphere** — the rigid crust plus the uppermost mantle, broken
  into tectonic plates about 100 km thick on average.
- **Asthenosphere** — the hotter, weaker, partly molten zone beneath it
  (extending to roughly 400 km); the main source of magma and the layer
  on which the plates slide.

[info title="Two schemes, one planet"]
Crust–mantle–core describes *what the layers are made of*;
lithosphere–asthenosphere describes *how they behave* under stress.
Both divisions are used side by side in plate tectonics.
[/info]

## 7. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Deepest mine? | Mponeng, South Africa (~4 km) |
| Deepest borehole? | Kola (~12 km) |
| Most important evidence? | Seismic waves |
| Moho (1909) separates? | Crust from mantle |
| Gutenberg discontinuity? | Mantle–core boundary (~2,900 km) |
| Lehmann (1936) identified? | The solid inner core (~5,100 km) |
| Outer core's state? | Liquid — S-waves cannot cross it |
